## Getting started

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/four-rooms-q-learning.git
cd four-rooms-q-learning
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Run any scenario (deterministic actions by default):

```bash
python Scenario1.py
python Scenario2.py
python Scenario3.py
```

Add `-stochastic` for noisy actions:

```bash
python Scenario1.py -stochastic
```

Figures are saved to `plots/`. Scenario 1 saves the exploration comparison curves
(mean reward with a standard-deviation band over runs) and the final path for each
strategy. Scenarios 2 and 3 save the final path of the last run.

## Limitations

- Package locations are fixed within a run, so the Q-table learns one layout rather
  than a general policy.
- In Scenario 2, the state records how many packages remain but not which ones,
  so different situations can look identical to the agent.
- Reward curves average runs that each have a different random layout.

